<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barbados Slave Code</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1661</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Barbados Slave Code of 1661, formally An Act for the better ordering and governing of Negroes, was a law passed by the colonial assembly of Barbados to regulate enslaved Africans. It treated the enslaved as property, imposed harsh punishments and gave masters broad powers with few protections. It served as a model for slave codes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, Antigua and other English colonies.</p></div>
